 "indicator": {
  "definition": {
   "definition": "RBI publishes the credit-deposit ratio in the 'Ratios and Rates' table of its Weekly Statistical Supplement (and in the Monthly Bulletin); its compilation is described in the Guide to the RBI Monthly Bulletin and the Handbook on the WSS rather than in a statutory instrument. The outstanding ratio = total bank credit divided by aggregate deposits (demand plus time), expressed as a percentage — the share of the deposit base deployed as loans. The incremental credit-deposit ratio = incremental advances divided by incremental deposits, where the increments relate to the financial year so far, so a reading above 100 per cent means new lending exceeds fresh deposit mobilisation and the gap must be funded from non-deposit sources. RBI's September 2026 Bulletin article records the outstanding ratio rising from 68.6 per cent (September 2021) to 82.2 per cent (March 2026) and cautions that the ratio alone is not an appropriate measure of a banking system's funding vulnerability.",
   "source_name": "Reserve Bank of India — Guide to the RBI Monthly Bulletin; Weekly Statistical Supplement 'Ratios'; RBI Bulletin, September 2026",
   "source_url": "https://www.rbi.org.in/Scripts/PublicationsView.aspx?id=17618",
   "term": "Credit-deposit ratio (outstanding and incremental)"
